{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23 && nl -ba packets.md | sed -n '1294,1351p;1355,1405p;1653,1684p;2357,2406p;2410,2455p' | cut -c 1-1400\"","aggregated_output":"  1294\t### Card K1: How much will NVIDIA's equity investment commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report?\n  1295\t  Ladder: rung 1: Commitments are below $25 billion. / rung 2: Commitments are at least $25 billion and below $50 billion. / rung 3: Commitments are at least $50 billion and below $75 billion. / rung 4: Commitments are at least $75 billion and below $100 billion. / rung 5: Commitments are at least $100 billion.\n  1296\t  Passages, as the request showed them:\n  1297\t  - e1732 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya ; from gnews:d024d71d75661d369798d307 2026-08-12] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya said NVIDIA had committed to provide $70 billion in equity investments to ecosystem partners; the period covered was not specified.\n  1298\t    Quote: Nvidia has committed to directly providing $70 billion in equity investments into ecosystem partners, per new research from Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya.\n  1299\t  - e1919 [rumour; Yahoo Finance; original source The Information 2026-08-07; from gnews:e2a48863c597e02d402f1551 2026-08-16] The Information reported on August 7, 2026, that NVIDIA planned to invest up to $3 billion in Lancium: an initial $2 billion for about 20% equity, plus up to $1 billion tied mainly to securing additional grid interconnections.\n  1300\t    Quote: According to a report from The Information, published on August 7, NVIDIA will spend up to $3 billion in Lancium, a power infrastructure developer and the firm behind the Stargate data center project in Abilene, Texas. The deal is structured in two parts: an initial $2 billion for a roughly 20% equity share in Lancium, followed by an additional $1 billion based on the company meeting particular go\n  1301\t  - e1957 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-17; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000069:sbeoainvidia-portsrelease.htm 2026-08-17] NVIDIA said it will invest $1.5 billion in SB Energy.\n  1302\t    Quote: NVIDIA will invest $1.5 billion in SB Energy, joining existing investors SoftBank Group and OpenAI.\n  1303\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2404; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1304\t  - e1989 [fact; CNBC; original source SB Energy, SoftBank, and NVIDIA ; from gnews:a95b9986d8697704ffa17843 2026-08-17] For the Ohio project, SB Energy and SoftBank plan power sources supporting 10 GW and at least $4.2 billion of regional grid investment; NVIDIA will invest $1.5 billion in SB Energy.\n  1305\t    Quote: As part of the Ohio deal, SB Energy and SoftBank will build power sources supporting 10 gigawatts of energy and invest at least $4.2 billion into regional grid infrastructure. Nvidia will invest $1.5 billion in SB Energy.\n  1306\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1984, e1999, e2022, e2045; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1307\t  - e2404 [fact; Latitude Media; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:74b684fc710dce162d8f4fe1 2026-08-24] NVIDIA announced a strategic investment in Lancium and a $1.5 billion investment in SB Energy in August 2026.\n  1308\t    Quote: Also this month, the company announced both a strategic investment in Lancium , a Blackstone-backed AI data center developer integrating grid interconnection, behind-the-meter generation, and storage, and a $1.5-billion investment in SB Energy , which builds “power-first” AI infrastructure.\n  1309\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1957; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1310\t  - e2620 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000075:nvda-20260726.htm 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported $99 billion of equity investments and $25 billion of equity investment commitments as of 2026-07-26.\n  1311\t    Quote: These include equity investments of $99 billion and equity investment commitments of $25 billion as of July 26, 2026.\n  1312\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2929, e3258, e3477, e3889, e3917; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1313\t  - e2643 [party_statement; Jensen Huang, quoted in the CNBC interview transcript; original source Jensen Huang 2026-08-26; from extra:b23f73a08a535993119430f5 2026-08-26] Jensen Huang said NVIDIA’s large investments in AI labs in the current year were not expected to repeat next year.\n  1314\t    Quote: And those large investments in AI labs are not going to repeat.\n  1315\t  - e3474 [fact; insidermonkey.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:baa07a74334dc91adc7d85bc 2026-08-31] NVIDIA is investing $3.5 billion in MediaTek through convertible bonds.\n  1316\t    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is investing $3.5 billion in Taiwan-based MediaTek through convertible bonds, taking an existing AI partnership a step further.\n  1317\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3558; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1318\t  - e3558 [fact; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:0849c71e57d8603a7cbf08e0 2026-08-31] NVIDIA announced it would buy US$3.5 billion of MediaTek bonds convertible into shares; the deal was announced Monday.\n  1319\t    Quote: The deal announced on Monday will see Nvidia buy bonds convertible into MediaTek shares.\n  1320\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3474; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1321\t  - e3880 [fact; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:08b1b514a1f4b7067e0dcda4 2026-09-04] NVIDIA said it committed over $40 billion to financing rounds across the AI stack in 2026.\n  1322\t    Quote: The company has ramped up dealmaking for financing rounds across the AI stack in the previous 12 months, with over $40 billion committed in 2026.\n  1323\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3890; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1324\t  - e3934 [rumour; finance.yahoo.com; original source finance.yahoo.com ; from gnews:67a905d722fae62095fd741e 2026-09-04] NVIDIA is reportedly discussing an approximately $2.5 billion investment in Thinking Machines Lab; no completion date is stated.\n  1325\t    Quote: Nvidia is separately in discussions to invest approximately $2.5 billion\n  1326\t  - e4648 [rumour; Calcalist Tech, citing Reuters; original source Unnamed people familiar with the discussions cited by Reuters ; from gnews:767d795fbdf40bc90ce11e15 2026-09-14] People familiar with discussions told Reuters that Nvidia could invest up to $10 billion in Anthropic’s potential IPO.\n  1327\t    Quote: Nvidia could invest as much as $10 billion\n  1328\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4669; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1329\t  - e4669 [rumour; InvestingNews; original source Reuters ; from gnews:65814ac898f65c6b360be321 2026-09-14] Reuters reported that NVIDIA is weighing a US$10 billion investment commitment in Anthropic's IPO; no signing date is stated.\n  1330\t    Quote: According to a Reuters exclusive , the chipmaker is weighing a US$10 billion commitment to the offering, serving as an early backer that could signal strong institutional confidence to public markets preparing to absorb the astronomical valuations and capital demands of frontier artificial intelligence labs.\n  1331\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4648; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1332\t  - e4729 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6261d9e9a1fe8d22c05f5e82 2026-09-15] NVIDIA spent more than $42 billion buying equity stakes in other companies in the first half of FY2027, versus about $1.2 billion in the prior-year first half.\n  1333\t    Quote: Nvidia spent more than $42 billion buying equity stakes in other companies in the first half of the fiscal year, up from about $1.2 billion a year earlier.\n  1334\t  Figures (declared):\n  1335\t  - {\"id\": \"F1\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"equity investment commitments\", \"scope\": \"company\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q2 quarter-end (2026-07-26)\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"reported\", \"value\": 25, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"quote_number\": \"$25 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"evidence\": \"e2620\"}\n  1336\t  - {\"id\": \"F2\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"investment in SB Energy\", \"scope\": \"company investment\", \"period\": \"announced August 2026\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"reported\", \"value\": 1.5, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"quote_number\": \"$1.5 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"evidence\": \"e1957\"}\n  1337\t  - {\"id\": \"F3\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"investment in convertible MediaTek bonds\", \"scope\": \"bonds convertible into shares\", \"period\": \"announced August 2026\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"reported\", \"value\": 3.5, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"quote_number\": \"$3.5 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"evidence\": \"e3474\"}\n  1338\t  - {\"id\": \"F4\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"initial investment in Lancium\", \"scope\": \"equity investment tranche\", \"period\": \"reported as under discussion August 2026\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"third_party\", \"value\": 2, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"quote_number\": \"$2 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"evidence\": \"e1919\"}\n  1339\t  - {\"id\": \"F5\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"potential investment in Anthropic\", \"scope\": \"potential IPO investment\", \"period\": \"timing not stated\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"third_party\", \"value\": 10, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"quote_number\": \"$10 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"as much as\", \"evidence\": \"e4648\"}\n  1340\t  - {\"id\": \"F6\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"potential investment in Thinking Machines Lab\", \"scope\": \"investment under discussion\", \"period\": \"completion date not stated\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"third_party\", \"value\": 2.5, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"quote_number\": \"$2.5 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"approximately\", \"evidence\": \"e3934\"}\n  1341\t  - {\"id\": \"F7\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"financing-round commitments\", \"scope\": \"across the AI stack\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"reported\", \"value\": 40, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"quote_number\": \"$40 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"over\", \"evidence\": \"e3880\"}\n  1342\t  - {\"id\": \"F8\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"equity stakes purchased\", \"scope\": \"other companies\", \"period\": \"FY2027 H1\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"reported\", \"value\": 42, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"quote_number\": \"$42 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"more than\", \"evidence\": \"e4729\"}\n  1343\t  - {\"id\": \"F9\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"incremental equity investment commitments\", \"scope\": \"company\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q3-Q4\"}, \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 2, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"basis_text\": \"I estimate the Lancium contribution at the reported initial tranche rather than the larger possible total: the report describes an initial investment and a further contingent amount, while NVIDIA later confirmed a strategic investment without sizing it. This is not a confirmed closing or a company-reported commitment balance.\", \"rests_on\": [\"F4\", \"e1919\", \"e2404\"]}\n  1344\t  - {\"id\": \"F10\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"incremental equity investment commitments\", \"scope\": \"company\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q3-Q4\"}, \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 5, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"basis_text\": \"I use half of the reported possible Anthropic amount as a probability-weighted estimate: the report gives a possible IPO investment but no signing date, so taking the full amount into the FY2027 balance would overstate what is in hand. The estimate is not a reported deal size or commitment.\", \"rests_on\": [\"F5\", \"e4648\", \"e4669\"]}\n  1345\t  - {\"id\": \"F11\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"incremental equity investment commitments\", \"scope\": \"company\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q3-Q4\"}, \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 1.25, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"basis_text\": \"I use half of the approximate amount under discussion as a probability-weighted allowance. The report gives neither a completion date nor evidence of a signed investment, so I do not count the full potential amount.\", \"rests_on\": [\"F6\", \"e3934\"]}\n  1346\t  - {\"id\": \"F12\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"other incremental equity investment commitments\", \"scope\": \"company\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q3-Q4\"}, \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 6.75, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"basis_text\": \"I allow for further net commitments beyond the individually identified transactions, but at a moderated pace: NVIDIA reported substantial financing activity in 2026 and very large equity purchases in FY2027 H1, while Jensen Huang said large AI-lab investments were not expected to repeat next year. These broad activity figures are context, not amounts to add to this balance; the forecast allowance is my estimate for the remaining FY2027 quarters.\", \"rests_on\": [\"F7\", \"F8\", \"e2643\", \"e3880\", \"e4729\"]}\n  1347\t  - {\"id\": \"F13\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"incremental equity investment commitments\", \"scope\": \"company\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q3-Q4\"}, \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 20, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"inputs\": [\"F2\", \"F3\", \"F9\", \"F10\", \"F11\", \"F12\"], \"operation\": \"sum\"}\n  1348\t  - {\"id\": \"F14\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"equity investment commitments\", \"scope\": \"company\", \"period\": \"end of FY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 45, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F13\"], \"operation\": \"sum\"}\n  1349\t  Recomputed outcome: [45.0, 45.0, null]\n  1350\t  Recorded assumptions: [{\"type\": \"qualified source\", \"figure\": \"F5\", \"qualifier\": \"as much as\"}, {\"type\": \"qualified source\", \"figure\": \"F6\", \"qualifier\": \"approximately\"}, {\"type\": \"qualified source\", \"figure\": \"F7\", \"qualifier\": \"over\"}, {\"type\": \"qualified source\", \"figure\": \"F8\", \"qualifier\": \"more than\"}, {\"type\": \"estimate\", \"figure\": \"F9\", \"assumption\": \"I estimate the Lancium contribution at the reported initial tranche rather than the larger possible total: the report describes an initial investment and a further contingent amount, while NVIDIA later confirmed a strategic investment without sizing it. This is not a confirmed closing or a company-reported commitment balance.\"}, {\"type\": \"estimate\", \"figure\": \"F10\", \"assumption\": \"I use half of the reported possible Anthropic amount as a probability-weighted estimate: the report gives a possible IPO investment but no signing date, so taking the full amount into the FY2027 balance would overstate what is in hand. The estimate is not a reported deal size or commitment.\"}, {\"type\": \"estimate\", \"figure\": \"F11\", \"assumption\": \"I use half of the approximate amount under discussion as a probability-weighted allowance. The report gives neither a completion date nor evidence of a signed investment, so I do not count the full potential amount.\"}, {\"type\": \"estimate\", \"figure\": \"F12\", \"assumption\": \"I allow for further net \n  1351\t  Card K1: {\"view_id\": \"v29\", \"shape\": \"forecast\", \"rung\": 2, \"reasons\": \"The reported balance was $25 billion at FY2027 Q2 quarter-end. I forecast about $20 billion of net additional commitments through FY2027 Q4, giving an estimated year-end balance of $45 billion and rung 2. The estimate includes the sized SB Energy investment and convertible MediaTek bonds, a conservative allowance for the announced-but-unsized Lancium investment, probability-weighted allowances for the potential Anthropic and Thinking Machines investments, and a modest allowance for other activity. The two potential lab investments are not certain to close in time; conversely, further undisclosed commitments could push the balance higher. Rung 3 requires at least $5 billion more than this point estimate. I judge rung 2 more likely because the Anthropic amount is only being weighed with no signing date, Thinking Machines is also only under discussion, and management said large AI-lab investments were not expected to repeat next year. The high 2026 deal activity and first-half equity purchases argue against simply holding the balance flat, but they are not equivalent to the end-period commitments balance and do not justify carrying their pace forward unchanged. The broad $70 billion outside estimate has no stated period and therefore does not establish the FY2027 outcome or serve as its reference.\", \"\n  1355\t### Card K1: What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?\n  1356\t  Ladder: rung 1: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $50 billion. / rung 2: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $40 billion and less than $50 billion. / rung 3: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion. / rung 4: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion. / rung 5: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is less than $20 billion.\n  1357\t  Passages, as the request showed them:\n  1358\t  - e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n  1359\t    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\n  1360\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3302, e4691, e4856; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1361\t  - e4687 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source AMD ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] AMD estimates that server CPU market revenue could grow at a 50% annual rate through 2030, reaching a $220 billion opportunity by the end of the decade.\n  1362\t    Quote: AMD notes that the server CPU market's revenue could increase at an annual rate of 50% through 2030, creating a $220 billion revenue opportunity by the end of the decade.\n  1363\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4084; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1364\t  - e4689 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), quoting Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera CPU deployments would include every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\n  1365\t    Quote: Kress added that the Vera CPU will be deployed by \"every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\"\n  1366\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1367\t  - e4691 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 to be double its FY2027 level.\n  1368\t    Quote: What's more, Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year.\n  1369\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660, e4764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1370\t  - e4692 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst), citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] Tom's Hardware reported that Arm-based servers represented 45% of data-center market revenue share in June.\n  1371\t    Quote: Tom's Hardware noted in June that Arm-based servers account for 45% of the data center market's revenue share.\n  1372\t  - e4764 [party_statement; fool.com (Keith Noonan); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:0ba93692dc5df4b999fe3729 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang forecast that NVIDIA CPU revenue would more than double in FY2028.\n  1373\t    Quote: Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year\n  1374\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4691; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1375\t  - e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n  1376\t    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n  1377\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1378\t  - e4857 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Bank of America analysts ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] Bank of America analysts forecast that the CPU market would grow fivefold, from $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030.\n  1379\t    Quote: Analysts at Bank of America also released a research report in June, saying they believe the CPU market will grow fivefold between this year and 2030, from $35 billion to $170 billion.\n  1380\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e935; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1381\t  - e5044 [party_statement; Insider Monkey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a1af1bb8840fa4ec9e52bcb0 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said Anthropic is evaluating NVIDIA’s Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads.\n  1382\t    Quote: Nvidia recently said Anthropic is evaluating its Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads,\n  1383\t  - e5399 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs delivered 20% higher per-core performance than NVIDIA’s 88-core Vera CPU in SPEC 2026.\n  1384\t    Quote: Here, the AMD 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs provide a 20% uplift over NVIDIA's 88-Core Vera CPU.\n  1385\t  - e5400 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9996 delivered 1.2 times Vera-based platform per-core performance and 2.24 times its platform-level performance in SPECrate 2026 Integer testing.\n  1386\t    Quote: In SPECrate 2026 Integer testing, the AMD EPYC 9996 server CPU delivers 1.2 times the per-core performance of an Nvidia Vera-based platform and 2.24 times its platform-level performance.\n  1387\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5443; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1388\t  - e5401 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD estimated that an EPYC 9996 system in a modeled 100-kilowatt rack would deliver 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n  1389\t    Quote: In a modeled 100-kilowatt rack, it delivers an estimated 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n  1390\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5444; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1391\t  - e5402 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores had a 37% performance gain over NVIDIA Vera, while its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores had a 2.37-times gain in the overall SPEC 2026 suite.\n  1392\t    Quote: Here, the 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores provide a 37% gain over NVIDIA Vera & the 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores provides a 2.37x gain.\n  1393\t  - e5403 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9006 server CPUs averaged 12% higher per-core performance in Agentic AI-specific benchmarks, with a4 subtests averaging 20%.\n  1394\t    Quote: Moving over to Agentic AI-specific benchmark tests, AMD's EPYC 9006 Server CPUs drive a 12% gain in per-core performance benchmarks, with the a4 subtests' performance averaging at 20%.\n  1395\t  - e5404 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that EPYC Venice had 18% faster memory bandwidth than NVIDIA Vera, with 1.6 TB/s versus Vera’s 1.2 TB/s, in STREAM Triad testing.\n  1396\t    Quote: AMD's EPYC Venice delivered 18% faster memory bandwidth versus NVIDIA's Vera thanks to its 16-channel memory design, which supports up to 12,800 MT/s RDIMM modules. This provides the platform with 1.6 TB/s of memory bandwidth versus 1.2 TB/s bandwidth on the NVIDIA Vera CPUs (using LPDDR5x).\n  1397\t  Figures (declared):\n  1398\t  - {\"id\": \"F1\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 20, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"quote_number\": \"$20 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"evidence\": \"e3660\"}\n  1399\t  - {\"id\": \"F2\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue growth multiple\", \"scope\": \"CPU revenue, FY2027 to FY2028\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 2, \"unit\": \"x\", \"quote_number\": \"double\", \"qualifier\": \"more than\", \"evidence\": \"e3660\"}\n  1400\t  - {\"id\": \"F3\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"CPU, FY2028 guidance threshold derived from FY2027 base\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"}, \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 40, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"], \"operation\": \"product\"}\n  1401\t  - {\"id\": \"F4\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU revenue from FY2027 to FY2028\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"}, \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 90, \"unit\": \"%\", \"basis_text\": \"My estimate discounts management's more-than-doubling target by 10 percentage points: the broad deployment statement is encouraging, but the evidence does not quantify orders or volumes in hand for FY2028, and AMD's reported competitive results add execution risk.\", \"rests_on\": [\"F1\", \"e3660\", \"e4689\", \"e4691\", \"e4856\", \"e5399\", \"e5400\", \"e5401\", \"e5402\", \"e5403\", \"e5404\"]}\n  1402\t  - {\"id\": \"F5\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"}, \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 38, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F4\"], \"operation\": \"apply_growth\"}\n  1403\t  Recomputed outcome: [38.0, 38.0, null]\n  1404\t  Recorded assumptions: [{\"type\": \"qualified source\", \"figure\": \"F2\", \"qualifier\": \"more than\"}, {\"type\": \"conversion between quantities\", \"figure\": \"F3\"}, {\"type\": \"carried across periods\", \"figure\": \"F3\"}, {\"type\": \"estimate\", \"figure\": \"F4\", \"assumption\": \"My estimate discounts management's more-than-doubling target by 10 percentage points: the broad deployment statement is encouraging, but the evidence does not quantify orders or volumes in hand for FY2028, and AMD's reported competitive results add execution risk.\"}, {\"type\": \"conversion between quantities\", \"figure\": \"F5\"}, {\"type\": \"carried across periods\", \"figure\": \"F5\"}]\n  1405\t  Card K1: {\"view_id\": \"v41\", \"shape\": \"forecast\", \"rung\": 3, \"reasons\": \"The market reference is NVIDIA's guidance that FY2028 CPU revenue will more than double the FY2027 $20 billion figure, putting its $40 billion threshold in rung 2 (F1–F3). That guidance is meaningful, but it is not evidence that the required Vera volumes are already secured. Management's broad deployment statement supports a substantial ramp, while Anthropic is only evaluating Vera and neither item sizes demand. AMD's reported competitive results are a risk, though they do not establish a revenue shortfall by themselves. On balance, I forecast $38 billion: a modest discount to the more-than-double guidance threshold, reflecting the unquantified volume and execution risk. That places the outcome in rung 3, below $40 billion and above $30 billion.\", \"cites\": [\"e3660\", \"e4687\", \"e4689\", \"e4691\", \"e4692\", \"e4764\", \"e4856\", \"e4857\", \"e5044\", \"e5399\", \"e5400\", \"e5401\", \"e5402\", \"e5403\", \"e5404\"], \"pushes\": [{\"evidence_id\": \"e3660\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4687\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4689\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4691\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4692\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4764\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4856\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4857\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_i\n  1653\t### Card K1: How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\n  1654\t  Ladder: rung 1: Combined spending is less than $800 billion. / rung 2: Combined spending is at least $800 billion and less than $900 billion. / rung 3: Combined spending is at least $900 billion and less than $1.1 trillion. / rung 4: Combined spending is at least $1.1 trillion and less than $1.2 trillion. / rung 5: Combined spending is at least $1.2 trillion.\n  1655\t  Passages, as the request showed them:\n  1656\t  - e1076 [party_statement; moomoo.com author, reporting Alphabet’s statement; original source Alphabet ; from gnews:db5c4d64cdbdd661f0e35836 2026-07-27] Alphabet said it would increase capital expenditures further in 2027.\n  1657\t    Quote: and said it would increase further next year.\n  1658\t  - e1324 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, quoting Microsoft; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:ceaa6331ac2712fff502d347 2026-07-30] Microsoft said it added one gigawatt of capacity during the quarter and was on track to double its footprint in two years.\n  1659\t    Quote: Microsoft added “another gigawatt of capacity this quarter” and is on track to double its footprint in just two years.\n  1660\t  - e1366 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Microsoft CFO Amy Hood 2026-07-29; from gnews:c3795c657a584ee726778e8f 2026-08-01] Microsoft CFO Amy Hood said Microsoft’s fiscal 2027 capex will grow year over year, citing demand signals across its portfolio.\n  1661\t    Quote: chief financial officer Amy Hood said that for fiscal 2027, capex will grow year over year due to \"demand signals across our portfolio.\"\n  1662\t  - e1926 [third_party_view; uk.finance.yahoo.com, citing Bank of America; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Bank of America said analyst consensus for hyperscalers’ calendar 2027 spending rose from $480 billion in August 2025 to $1.08 trillion in August 2026, an increase of about 127%.\n  1663\t    Quote: What analysts expect the hyperscalers to spend in 2027 alone has more than doubled in the space of a year, from a consensus of $480 billion (€416bn) in August 2025 to $1.08 trillion (€943bn) this month, a rise of about 127%, according to Bank of America.\n  1664\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1559, e2008; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1665\t  - e2259 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source UBS ; from gnews:822e18e27e42a11a5a7c692a 2026-08-22] UBS data cited by the article projects hyperscaler capital expenditure growth of 25% in calendar 2027 and 6% in calendar 2028.\n  1666\t    Quote: For instance, data from UBS shows that growth in hyperscaler capital expenditure growth can slow down to 25% in 2027 and 6% in 2028.\n  1667\t  - e2535 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said cloud-industry backlog exceeded $2 trillion and top-five hyperscaler capital expenditures were expected to approach $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n  1668\t    Quote: With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n  1669\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e465, e3124, e3306, e3307, e3372, e3401, e3405, e3485, e4112; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  1670\t  - e3391 [third_party_view; Ellen Hazen, F.L. Putnam Investment Management chief market strategist and portfolio manager, quoted by Yahoo Finance; original source Ellen Hazen ; from gnews:9f03bbc66269c49a5ec8afee 2026-08-30] Ellen Hazen of F.L. Putnam said hyperscaler capital expenditures expected to exceed $1 trillion in calendar 2027 may not all be spent, citing labor, permitting, local-opposition and component constraints.\n  1671\t    Quote: “The biggest risk is whether or not the trillion dollars in hyperscaler capex next year can actually be spent — we have labor shortages, we have permitting delays, we have NIMBY increasing in volume, and we have component shortages,” Hazen said.\n  1672\t  - e3645 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Texas officials ; from gnews:b7a72f34e62112b669d71e36 2026-09-01] Texas has frozen new grid connections while officials audit developers.\n  1673\t    Quote: Texas has frozen new grid connections while officials audit developers.\n  1674\t  - e3646 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Utilities in other markets ; from gnews:b7a72f34e62112b669d71e36 2026-09-01] Utilities in other markets are demanding upfront cash or proof that developers control proposed data-center sites.\n  1675\t    Quote: Utilities in other markets are demanding cash upfront or proof that proposed sites are actually controlled.\n  1676\t  - e46 [third_party_view; Paul Triolo, quoted by CNBC; original source Paul Triolo ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] DGA-Albright Stonebridge Group partner Paul Triolo said power supply was likely to remain the main constraint on U.S. AI data-center spending and suggested advanced-system delays could coincide with the easing of power bottlenecks.\n  1677\t    Quote: With power supply likely to remain the major constraint on American AI data center spending, Triolo said, \"delays in getting to more advanced systems could just mean that the new systems are ready by the time the U.S. can work to overcome some of the critical bottlenecks on power now dogging the industry.\"\n  1678\t  Figures (declared):\n  1679\t  - {\"id\": \"F1\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"capital expenditures\", \"scope\": \"combined; consensus reference, not data-center-only\", \"period\": \"CY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"consensus\", \"value\": 1080, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"quote_number\": \"$1.08 trillion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"evidence\": \"e1926\"}\n  1680\t  - {\"id\": \"F2\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center share of capital expenditures\", \"scope\": \"share of the CY2027 combined-capex reference\", \"period\": \"CY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 88, \"unit\": \"%\", \"basis_text\": \"My estimate is that 88% of the combined capex reference is data-center capex. I use 85–90% as a reasonable sensitivity range: the cited evidence is heavily focused on AI infrastructure and data-center capacity, but the reference is total capex and the passages do not quantify the data-center portion. The range implies about $918–972bn using F1, which remains in rung 3.\", \"rests_on\": [\"F1\", \"e2535\", \"e1324\", \"e3645\", \"e3646\"]}\n  1681\t  - {\"id\": \"F3\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center capex\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 950.4, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"], \"operation\": \"share\"}\n  1682\t  Recomputed outcome: [950.4, 950.4, null]\n  1683\t  Recorded assumptions: [{\"type\": \"estimate\", \"figure\": \"F2\", \"assumption\": \"My estimate is that 88% of the combined capex reference is data-center capex. I use 85\\u201390% as a reasonable sensitivity range: the cited evidence is heavily focused on AI infrastructure and data-center capacity, but the reference is total capex and the passages do not quantify the data-center portion. The range implies about $918\\u2013972bn using F1, which remains in rung 3.\"}]\n  1684\t  Card K1: {\"view_id\": \"v6\", \"shape\": \"forecast\", \"rung\": 3, \"reasons\": \"I forecast about $950bn of data-center capex: the $1.08tn consensus is a useful market reference for hyperscaler capex, but it is not itself the answer because it is not data-center-only. Applying my estimated 88% data-center share gives $950.4bn; a plausible 85–90% share gives about $918–972bn, still within rung 3. NVIDIA’s $1.3tn top-five capex projection is a materially higher alternative, but it covers five companies and all capex, and is an outside projection rather than a quantified commitment of data-center spending by the four in the question. The evidence of further capex growth and capacity additions supports substantial spend, while power, grid-connection, permitting and component constraints argue against simply taking the highest projection as delivered spend. On balance, the consensus anchor adjusted to the requested scope is more likely than the high top-five projection to represent the outcome.\", \"cites\": [\"e1926\", \"e2535\", \"e1076\", \"e1324\", \"e1366\", \"e2259\", \"e3391\", \"e3645\", \"e3646\", \"e46\"], \"pushes\": [{\"evidence_id\": \"e1926\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e2535\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1076\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1324\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1366\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e2259\", \"push\": \"towar\n  2357\t### Card K1: How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\n  2358\t  Ladder: rung 1: Combined spending is less than $800 billion. / rung 2: Combined spending is at least $800 billion and less than $900 billion. / rung 3: Combined spending is at least $900 billion and less than $1.1 trillion. / rung 4: Combined spending is at least $1.1 trillion and less than $1.2 trillion. / rung 5: Combined spending is at least $1.2 trillion.\n  2359\t  Passages, as the request showed them:\n  2360\t  - e1076 [party_statement; moomoo.com author, reporting Alphabet’s statement; original source Alphabet ; from gnews:db5c4d64cdbdd661f0e35836 2026-07-27] Alphabet said it would increase capital expenditures further in 2027.\n  2361\t    Quote: and said it would increase further next year.\n  2362\t  - e1324 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, quoting Microsoft; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:ceaa6331ac2712fff502d347 2026-07-30] Microsoft said it added one gigawatt of capacity during the quarter and was on track to double its footprint in two years.\n  2363\t    Quote: Microsoft added “another gigawatt of capacity this quarter” and is on track to double its footprint in just two years.\n  2364\t  - e1366 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Microsoft CFO Amy Hood 2026-07-29; from gnews:c3795c657a584ee726778e8f 2026-08-01] Microsoft CFO Amy Hood said Microsoft’s fiscal 2027 capex will grow year over year, citing demand signals across its portfolio.\n  2365\t    Quote: chief financial officer Amy Hood said that for fiscal 2027, capex will grow year over year due to \"demand signals across our portfolio.\"\n  2366\t  - e1920 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:e2a48863c597e02d402f1551 2026-08-16] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said returns on AI infrastructure investment had reset significantly over the prior six months and were now profitable at a scale that had calmed earlier skeptics.\n  2367\t    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) CEO Jensen Huang made the basic logic clear in remarks at a private gathering of family offices and financial firms in Taipei, arguing that returns on AI infrastructure investment have reset significantly over the last six months and are now profitable at a scale that has calmed earlier skeptics.\n  2368\t  - e1926 [third_party_view; uk.finance.yahoo.com, citing Bank of America; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Bank of America said analyst consensus for hyperscalers’ calendar 2027 spending rose from $480 billion in August 2025 to $1.08 trillion in August 2026, an increase of about 127%.\n  2369\t    Quote: What analysts expect the hyperscalers to spend in 2027 alone has more than doubled in the space of a year, from a consensus of $480 billion (€416bn) in August 2025 to $1.08 trillion (€943bn) this month, a rise of about 127%, according to Bank of America.\n  2370\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1559, e2008; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  2371\t  - e2259 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source UBS ; from gnews:822e18e27e42a11a5a7c692a 2026-08-22] UBS data cited by the article projects hyperscaler capital expenditure growth of 25% in calendar 2027 and 6% in calendar 2028.\n  2372\t    Quote: For instance, data from UBS shows that growth in hyperscaler capital expenditure growth can slow down to 25% in 2027 and 6% in 2028.\n  2373\t  - e3391 [third_party_view; Ellen Hazen, F.L. Putnam Investment Management chief market strategist and portfolio manager, quoted by Yahoo Finance; original source Ellen Hazen ; from gnews:9f03bbc66269c49a5ec8afee 2026-08-30] Ellen Hazen of F.L. Putnam said hyperscaler capital expenditures expected to exceed $1 trillion in calendar 2027 may not all be spent, citing labor, permitting, local-opposition and component constraints.\n  2374\t    Quote: “The biggest risk is whether or not the trillion dollars in hyperscaler capex next year can actually be spent — we have labor shortages, we have permitting delays, we have NIMBY increasing in volume, and we have component shortages,” Hazen said.\n  2375\t  - e3457 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com; original source Bernstein analyst Stacy Rasgon and other Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:7a5939361ddb9a65196dec06 2026-08-30] Bernstein analyst Stacy Rasgon and other Wall Street analysts warned that chipmakers’ investments in downstream customers who buy chips are amplifying concerns about the sustainability of AI capital expenditure.\n  2376\t    Quote: Bernstein analyst Stacy Rasgon and other Wall Street analysts warn that the \"circular financing\" pattern, in which chipmakers invest in downstream customers who then use that money to buy chips, is amplifying market concerns about the sustainability of AI capital expenditure.\n  2377\t  - e3645 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Texas officials ; from gnews:b7a72f34e62112b669d71e36 2026-09-01] Texas has frozen new grid connections while officials audit developers.\n  2378\t    Quote: Texas has frozen new grid connections while officials audit developers.\n  2379\t  - e3646 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Utilities in other markets ; from gnews:b7a72f34e62112b669d71e36 2026-09-01] Utilities in other markets are demanding upfront cash or proof that developers control proposed data-center sites.\n  2380\t    Quote: Utilities in other markets are demanding cash upfront or proof that proposed sites are actually controlled.\n  2381\t  - e4389 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author Ryan Vanzo, quoting Rich Privorotsky; original source Rich Privorotsky, Goldman Sachs analyst ; from gnews:b5313a0ca53ce1af48ab5c6c 2026-09-10] Goldman Sachs analyst Rich Privorotsky said GPT-6’s improvement in intelligence could shift the AI demand curve, prompt other labs to catch up, and sustain AI infrastructure spending.\n  2382\t    Quote: \"Price decline is one thing, but a real breakthrough in intelligence will once again change the demand curve,\" Privorotsky explains. \"It will force all other labs to catch up, keep the spending cycle energized, and reactivate the belief that 'there is something better worth building.'\"\n  2383\t  - e4497 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Pablo Hernández de Cos 2026-09-10; from gnews:8e160db6032cc34164f45e59 2026-09-11] On September 10, 2026, BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said AI investment is increasingly financed with debt and private credit, and warned disappointing returns could trigger an investment pullback.\n  2384\t    Quote: In a speech in Mumbai, BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said the AI investment boom is increasingly financed \"not by earnings but through debt and, notably, through private credit,\" and warned that if returns to AI disappoint, a pullback in investment \"could turn today's capital expenditure boom into a bust.\"\n  2385\t  - e46 [third_party_view; Paul Triolo, quoted by CNBC; original source Paul Triolo ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] DGA-Albright Stonebridge Group partner Paul Triolo said power supply was likely to remain the main constraint on U.S. AI data-center spending and suggested advanced-system delays could coincide with the easing of power bottlenecks.\n  2386\t    Quote: With power supply likely to remain the major constraint on American AI data center spending, Triolo said, \"delays in getting to more advanced systems could just mean that the new systems are ready by the time the U.S. can work to overcome some of the critical bottlenecks on power now dogging the industry.\"\n  2387\t  - e4739 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source BofA Global Research ; from gnews:ca2f580191a4509ac38bc1cf 2026-09-15] BofA Global Research estimated that Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle could spend about $1.08 trillion collectively on capital expenditures in calendar 2027.\n  2388\t    Quote: before pushing that figure to $1.08 trillion in 2027.\n  2389\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e412; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  2390\t  - e4756 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\n  2391\t    Quote: Nvidia's own earnings call also highlighted the scale of the spending cycle. The company said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n  2392\tA\n  2393\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4095, e4780, e4789, e4990; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  2394\t  - e5026 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-19; from gnews:4ee5d8f73b7bcfc2f2111ad1 2026-09-19] The article projects the five largest AI hyperscalers’ data-center capital expenditures at $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\n  2395\t    Quote: Next year, that figure is projected to rise to $1.3 trillion.\n  2396\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4458; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  2397\t\n  2398\t\n  2399\t  Figures (declared):\n  2400\t  - {\"id\": \"F1\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"five largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center capex\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"third_party\", \"value\": 1300, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"quote_number\": \"$1.3 trillion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"evidence\": \"e5026\"}\n  2401\t  - {\"id\": \"F2\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"Oracle, the fifth company in the five-company group\", \"measure\": \"data-center capex\", \"scope\": \"Oracle\", \"period\": \"CY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 150, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"basis_text\": \"My estimate for the fifth company's share of the five-company data-center total. The passages identify Oracle as one of the five companies, but do not give its individual capex. I use a material, but minority, allocation rather than treating the five-company figure as the four-company answer.\", \"rests_on\": [\"F1\", \"e4739\"]}\n  2402\t  - {\"id\": \"F3\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center capex\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 1150, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"], \"operation\": \"difference\"}\n  2403\t  - {\"id\": \"F4\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"capital expenditures\", \"scope\": \"combined; market consensus, not limited to data-center capex\", \"period\": \"CY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"consensus\", \"value\": 1080, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"quote_number\": \"$1.08 trillion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"evidence\": \"e1926\"}\n  2404\t  Recomputed outcome: [1150.0, 1150.0, null]\n  2405\t  Recorded assumptions: [{\"type\": \"estimate\", \"figure\": \"F2\", \"assumption\": \"My estimate for the fifth company's share of the five-company data-center total. The passages identify Oracle as one of the five companies, but do not give its individual capex. I use a material, but minority, allocation rather than treating the five-company figure as the four-company answer.\"}, {\"type\": \"conversion between quantities\", \"figure\": \"F3\"}]\n  2406\t  Card K1: {\"view_id\": \"v6\", \"shape\": \"forecast\", \"rung\": 4, \"reasons\": \"The closest stated market reference is $1.08 trillion of CY2027 hyperscaler spending, which sits on rung 3; it is broader than the question because it is not specifically data-center capex. I do not use that reference as the forecast. A more specific, later estimate puts the five largest AI hyperscalers' CY2027 data-center capex at $1.3 trillion. The evidence also identifies Oracle among the five-company group. Subtracting my $150 billion estimate for Oracle gives a four-company forecast of $1.15 trillion, on rung 4. The Oracle deduction is the main uncertainty: if its data-center capex were more than $200 billion, the four-company result would fall below $1.1 trillion; if it were below $100 billion, the result would reach at least $1.2 trillion. The evidence does not quantify that company's amount, so the deduction is an explicit assumption rather than a reported fact. Power, grid-connection, permitting, labor, and component constraints argue against assuming every high spending projection will be delivered, but they do not provide a quantified cut that outweighs the specific five-company data-center estimate. The cited $1.3 trillion estimate is a forecast, not proof that the spending has already occurred.\", \"cites\": [\"e5026\", \"e4739\", \"e1926\", \"e4756\", \"e3391\", \"e3645\", \"e3646\", \"e46\", \"e3457\", \"e\n  2410\t### Card K1: How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\n  2411\t  Ladder: rung 1: Combined spending is less than $800 billion. / rung 2: Combined spending is at least $800 billion and less than $900 billion. / rung 3: Combined spending is at least $900 billion and less than $1.1 trillion. / rung 4: Combined spending is at least $1.1 trillion and less than $1.2 trillion. / rung 5: Combined spending is at least $1.2 trillion.\n  2412\t  Passages, as the request showed them:\n  2413\t  - e1076 [party_statement; moomoo.com author, reporting Alphabet’s statement; original source Alphabet ; from gnews:db5c4d64cdbdd661f0e35836 2026-07-27] Alphabet said it would increase capital expenditures further in 2027.\n  2414\t    Quote: and said it would increase further next year.\n  2415\t  - e1324 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, quoting Microsoft; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:ceaa6331ac2712fff502d347 2026-07-30] Microsoft said it added one gigawatt of capacity during the quarter and was on track to double its footprint in two years.\n  2416\t    Quote: Microsoft added “another gigawatt of capacity this quarter” and is on track to double its footprint in just two years.\n  2417\t  - e1366 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Microsoft CFO Amy Hood 2026-07-29; from gnews:c3795c657a584ee726778e8f 2026-08-01] Microsoft CFO Amy Hood said Microsoft’s fiscal 2027 capex will grow year over year, citing demand signals across its portfolio.\n  2418\t    Quote: chief financial officer Amy Hood said that for fiscal 2027, capex will grow year over year due to \"demand signals across our portfolio.\"\n  2419\t  - e1920 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:e2a48863c597e02d402f1551 2026-08-16] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said returns on AI infrastructure investment had reset significantly over the prior six months and were now profitable at a scale that had calmed earlier skeptics.\n  2420\t    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) CEO Jensen Huang made the basic logic clear in remarks at a private gathering of family offices and financial firms in Taipei, arguing that returns on AI infrastructure investment have reset significantly over the last six months and are now profitable at a scale that has calmed earlier skeptics.\n  2421\t  - e1926 [third_party_view; uk.finance.yahoo.com, citing Bank of America; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Bank of America said analyst consensus for hyperscalers’ calendar 2027 spending rose from $480 billion in August 2025 to $1.08 trillion in August 2026, an increase of about 127%.\n  2422\t    Quote: What analysts expect the hyperscalers to spend in 2027 alone has more than doubled in the space of a year, from a consensus of $480 billion (€416bn) in August 2025 to $1.08 trillion (€943bn) this month, a rise of about 127%, according to Bank of America.\n  2423\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1559, e2008; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  2424\t  - e2259 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source UBS ; from gnews:822e18e27e42a11a5a7c692a 2026-08-22] UBS data cited by the article projects hyperscaler capital expenditure growth of 25% in calendar 2027 and 6% in calendar 2028.\n  2425\t    Quote: For instance, data from UBS shows that growth in hyperscaler capital expenditure growth can slow down to 25% in 2027 and 6% in 2028.\n  2426\t  - e2535 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said cloud-industry backlog exceeded $2 trillion and top-five hyperscaler capital expenditures were expected to approach $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n  2427\t    Quote: With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n  2428\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e465, e3124, e3306, e3307, e3372, e3401, e3405, e3485, e4112; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  2429\t  - e3391 [third_party_view; Ellen Hazen, F.L. Putnam Investment Management chief market strategist and portfolio manager, quoted by Yahoo Finance; original source Ellen Hazen ; from gnews:9f03bbc66269c49a5ec8afee 2026-08-30] Ellen Hazen of F.L. Putnam said hyperscaler capital expenditures expected to exceed $1 trillion in calendar 2027 may not all be spent, citing labor, permitting, local-opposition and component constraints.\n  2430\t    Quote: “The biggest risk is whether or not the trillion dollars in hyperscaler capex next year can actually be spent — we have labor shortages, we have permitting delays, we have NIMBY increasing in volume, and we have component shortages,” Hazen said.\n  2431\t  - e3457 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com; original source Bernstein analyst Stacy Rasgon and other Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:7a5939361ddb9a65196dec06 2026-08-30] Bernstein analyst Stacy Rasgon and other Wall Street analysts warned that chipmakers’ investments in downstream customers who buy chips are amplifying concerns about the sustainability of AI capital expenditure.\n  2432\t    Quote: Bernstein analyst Stacy Rasgon and other Wall Street analysts warn that the \"circular financing\" pattern, in which chipmakers invest in downstream customers who then use that money to buy chips, is amplifying market concerns about the sustainability of AI capital expenditure.\n  2433\t  - e3645 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Texas officials ; from gnews:b7a72f34e62112b669d71e36 2026-09-01] Texas has frozen new grid connections while officials audit developers.\n  2434\t    Quote: Texas has frozen new grid connections while officials audit developers.\n  2435\t  - e3646 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Utilities in other markets ; from gnews:b7a72f34e62112b669d71e36 2026-09-01] Utilities in other markets are demanding upfront cash or proof that developers control proposed data-center sites.\n  2436\t    Quote: Utilities in other markets are demanding cash upfront or proof that proposed sites are actually controlled.\n  2437\t  - e4389 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author Ryan Vanzo, quoting Rich Privorotsky; original source Rich Privorotsky, Goldman Sachs analyst ; from gnews:b5313a0ca53ce1af48ab5c6c 2026-09-10] Goldman Sachs analyst Rich Privorotsky said GPT-6’s improvement in intelligence could shift the AI demand curve, prompt other labs to catch up, and sustain AI infrastructure spending.\n  2438\t    Quote: \"Price decline is one thing, but a real breakthrough in intelligence will once again change the demand curve,\" Privorotsky explains. \"It will force all other labs to catch up, keep the spending cycle energized, and reactivate the belief that 'there is something better worth building.'\"\n  2439\t  - e4485 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source NVIDIA and Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR 2026-08-10; from gnews:8e160db6032cc34164f45e59 2026-09-11] On August 10, NVIDIA partnered with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR on platforms targeting more than $500 billion in third-party AI-infrastructure capital.\n  2440\t    Quote: On August 10, Nvidia expanded its role further by partnering with six financial institutions—Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR—on platforms aimed at mobilizing more than $500 billion of third-party capital for AI infrastructure.\n  2441\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1671; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n  2442\t  - e4497 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Pablo Hernández de Cos 2026-09-10; from gnews:8e160db6032cc34164f45e59 2026-09-11] On September 10, 2026, BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said AI investment is increasingly financed with debt and private credit, and warned disappointing returns could trigger an investment pullback.\n  2443\t    Quote: In a speech in Mumbai, BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said the AI investment boom is increasingly financed \"not by earnings but through debt and, notably, through private credit,\" and warned that if returns to AI disappoint, a pullback in investment \"could turn today's capital expenditure boom into a bust.\"\n  2444\t  - e46 [third_party_view; Paul Triolo, quoted by CNBC; original source Paul Triolo ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] DGA-Albright Stonebridge Group partner Paul Triolo said power supply was likely to remain the main constraint on U.S. AI data-center spending and suggested advanced-system delays could coincide with the easing of power bottlenecks.\n  2445\t    Quote: With power supply likely to remain the major constraint on American AI data center spending, Triolo said, \"delays in getting to more advanced systems could just mean that the new systems are ready by the time the U.S. can work to overcome some of the critical bottlenecks on power now dogging the industry.\"\n  2446\t  Figures (declared):\n  2447\t  - {\"id\": \"F1\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"top five hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"capital expenditures\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"third_party\", \"value\": 1300, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"quote_number\": \"$1.3 trillion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"evidence\": \"e2535\"}\n  2448\t  - {\"id\": \"F2\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"share of top-five capital expenditures\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 90, \"unit\": \"%\", \"basis_text\": \"My estimate is that the four largest account for 90% of top-five capex. The four major cloud platforms likely represent most of the five-company total, while the fifth company is not negligible. This is an allocation assumption, not a figure stated in the evidence.\", \"rests_on\": [\"F1\", \"e2535\"]}\n  2449\t  - {\"id\": \"F3\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"capital expenditures\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 1170, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"], \"operation\": \"share\"}\n  2450\t  - {\"id\": \"F4\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center share of capital expenditures\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 82, \"unit\": \"%\", \"basis_text\": \"My estimate is that 82% of these companies' capex is data-center capex. The evidence concerns a large AI-infrastructure buildout, but total capex also includes spending outside data centers, so I do not count the full capex forecast as the quantity asked.\", \"rests_on\": [\"F3\", \"e2535\", \"e1926\"]}\n  2451\t  - {\"id\": \"F5\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center capex\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 959.4, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"inputs\": [\"F3\", \"F4\"], \"operation\": \"share\"}\n  2452\t  - {\"id\": \"F6\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"capital expenditures\", \"scope\": \"combined consensus estimate\", \"period\": \"CY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"consensus\", \"value\": 1080, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"quote_number\": \"$1.08 trillion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"evidence\": \"e1926\"}\n  2453\t  Recomputed outcome: [959.4, 959.4, null]\n  2454\t  Recorded assumptions: [{\"type\": \"estimate\", \"figure\": \"F2\", \"assumption\": \"My estimate is that the four largest account for 90% of top-five capex. The four major cloud platforms likely represent most of the five-company total, while the fifth company is not negligible. This is an allocation assumption, not a figure stated in the evidence.\"}, {\"type\": \"estimate\", \"figure\": \"F4\", \"assumption\": \"My estimate is that 82% of these companies' capex is data-center capex. The evidence concerns a large AI-infrastructure buildout, but total capex also includes spending outside data centers, so I do not count the full capex forecast as the quantity asked.\"}]\n  2455\t  Card K1: {\"view_id\": \"v6\", \"shape\": \"forecast\", \"rung\": 3, \"reasons\": \"The market reference is $1.08 trillion of CY2027 hyperscaler capex, which sits on rung 3, but it is broader than the requested four-company data-center quantity and is not itself my reason for staying there. NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion top-five capex estimate is a high, relevant starting point. Applying my estimates that the four largest account for 90% of that total and that 82% of their capex is data-center capex gives $959.4 billion, on rung 3. These shares are the main uncertainties: a smaller four-company share or a lower data-center share could put the result below $900 billion; near-total concentration in the four and a higher data-center share could put it above $1.1 trillion. On balance, the top-five estimate and the scale of the buildout favor rung 3, while the fact that the requested population and spending category are both narrower than the starting figure argues against simply taking the full $1.3 trillion. Power, grid-connection, permitting, labor and component constraints add downside risk, but the evidence does not quantify cancellations or a specific reduction sufficient to make a lower rung more likely.\", \"cites\": [\"e1926\", \"e2535\", \"e3391\", \"e3645\", \"e3646\", \"e46\", \"e1076\", \"e1324\", \"e1366\", \"e2259\", \"e1920\", \"e3457\", \"e4485\", \"e4497\", \"e4389\"], \"pushes\": [{\"evidence_id\": \"e1926\", \"pu\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23 && nl -ba packets.md | sed -n '1394,1405p;1653,1659p;1677,1684p'; cat reading/third.json; nl -ba ledger.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23 && nl -ba packets.md | sed -n '1394,1405p;1653,1659p;1677,1684p'; cat reading/third.json; nl -ba ledger.json\"","aggregated_output":"  1394\t    Quote: Moving over to Agentic AI-specific benchmark tests, AMD's EPYC 9006 Server CPUs drive a 12% gain in per-core performance benchmarks, with the a4 subtests' performance averaging at 20%.\n  1395\t  - e5404 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that EPYC Venice had 18% faster memory bandwidth than NVIDIA Vera, with 1.6 TB/s versus Vera’s 1.2 TB/s, in STREAM Triad testing.\n  1396\t    Quote: AMD's EPYC Venice delivered 18% faster memory bandwidth versus NVIDIA's Vera thanks to its 16-channel memory design, which supports up to 12,800 MT/s RDIMM modules. This provides the platform with 1.6 TB/s of memory bandwidth versus 1.2 TB/s bandwidth on the NVIDIA Vera CPUs (using LPDDR5x).\n  1397\t  Figures (declared):\n  1398\t  - {\"id\": \"F1\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 20, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"quote_number\": \"$20 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"evidence\": \"e3660\"}\n  1399\t  - {\"id\": \"F2\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue growth multiple\", \"scope\": \"CPU revenue, FY2027 to FY2028\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 2, \"unit\": \"x\", \"quote_number\": \"double\", \"qualifier\": \"more than\", \"evidence\": \"e3660\"}\n  1400\t  - {\"id\": \"F3\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"CPU, FY2028 guidance threshold derived from FY2027 base\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"}, \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 40, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"], \"operation\": \"product\"}\n  1401\t  - {\"id\": \"F4\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU revenue from FY2027 to FY2028\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"}, \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 90, \"unit\": \"%\", \"basis_text\": \"My estimate discounts management's more-than-doubling target by 10 percentage points: the broad deployment statement is encouraging, but the evidence does not quantify orders or volumes in hand for FY2028, and AMD's reported competitive results add execution risk.\", \"rests_on\": [\"F1\", \"e3660\", \"e4689\", \"e4691\", \"e4856\", \"e5399\", \"e5400\", \"e5401\", \"e5402\", \"e5403\", \"e5404\"]}\n  1402\t  - {\"id\": \"F5\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"}, \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 38, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F4\"], \"operation\": \"apply_growth\"}\n  1403\t  Recomputed outcome: [38.0, 38.0, null]\n  1404\t  Recorded assumptions: [{\"type\": \"qualified source\", \"figure\": \"F2\", \"qualifier\": \"more than\"}, {\"type\": \"conversion between quantities\", \"figure\": \"F3\"}, {\"type\": \"carried across periods\", \"figure\": \"F3\"}, {\"type\": \"estimate\", \"figure\": \"F4\", \"assumption\": \"My estimate discounts management's more-than-doubling target by 10 percentage points: the broad deployment statement is encouraging, but the evidence does not quantify orders or volumes in hand for FY2028, and AMD's reported competitive results add execution risk.\"}, {\"type\": \"conversion between quantities\", \"figure\": \"F5\"}, {\"type\": \"carried across periods\", \"figure\": \"F5\"}]\n  1405\t  Card K1: {\"view_id\": \"v41\", \"shape\": \"forecast\", \"rung\": 3, \"reasons\": \"The market reference is NVIDIA's guidance that FY2028 CPU revenue will more than double the FY2027 $20 billion figure, putting its $40 billion threshold in rung 2 (F1–F3). That guidance is meaningful, but it is not evidence that the required Vera volumes are already secured. Management's broad deployment statement supports a substantial ramp, while Anthropic is only evaluating Vera and neither item sizes demand. AMD's reported competitive results are a risk, though they do not establish a revenue shortfall by themselves. On balance, I forecast $38 billion: a modest discount to the more-than-double guidance threshold, reflecting the unquantified volume and execution risk. That places the outcome in rung 3, below $40 billion and above $30 billion.\", \"cites\": [\"e3660\", \"e4687\", \"e4689\", \"e4691\", \"e4692\", \"e4764\", \"e4856\", \"e4857\", \"e5044\", \"e5399\", \"e5400\", \"e5401\", \"e5402\", \"e5403\", \"e5404\"], \"pushes\": [{\"evidence_id\": \"e3660\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4687\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4689\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4691\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4692\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4764\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4856\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4857\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5044\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5399\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5400\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5401\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5402\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5403\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5404\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}], \"theme_pushes\": [], \"outcome\": \"F5\", \"reference\": {\"figure\": \"F3\"}}\n  1653\t### Card K1: How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\n  1654\t  Ladder: rung 1: Combined spending is less than $800 billion. / rung 2: Combined spending is at least $800 billion and less than $900 billion. / rung 3: Combined spending is at least $900 billion and less than $1.1 trillion. / rung 4: Combined spending is at least $1.1 trillion and less than $1.2 trillion. / rung 5: Combined spending is at least $1.2 trillion.\n  1655\t  Passages, as the request showed them:\n  1656\t  - e1076 [party_statement; moomoo.com author, reporting Alphabet’s statement; original source Alphabet ; from gnews:db5c4d64cdbdd661f0e35836 2026-07-27] Alphabet said it would increase capital expenditures further in 2027.\n  1657\t    Quote: and said it would increase further next year.\n  1658\t  - e1324 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, quoting Microsoft; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:ceaa6331ac2712fff502d347 2026-07-30] Microsoft said it added one gigawatt of capacity during the quarter and was on track to double its footprint in two years.\n  1659\t    Quote: Microsoft added “another gigawatt of capacity this quarter” and is on track to double its footprint in just two years.\n  1677\t    Quote: With power supply likely to remain the major constraint on American AI data center spending, Triolo said, \"delays in getting to more advanced systems could just mean that the new systems are ready by the time the U.S. can work to overcome some of the critical bottlenecks on power now dogging the industry.\"\n  1678\t  Figures (declared):\n  1679\t  - {\"id\": \"F1\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"capital expenditures\", \"scope\": \"combined; consensus reference, not data-center-only\", \"period\": \"CY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"consensus\", \"value\": 1080, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"quote_number\": \"$1.08 trillion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"evidence\": \"e1926\"}\n  1680\t  - {\"id\": \"F2\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center share of capital expenditures\", \"scope\": \"share of the CY2027 combined-capex reference\", \"period\": \"CY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 88, \"unit\": \"%\", \"basis_text\": \"My estimate is that 88% of the combined capex reference is data-center capex. I use 85–90% as a reasonable sensitivity range: the cited evidence is heavily focused on AI infrastructure and data-center capacity, but the reference is total capex and the passages do not quantify the data-center portion. The range implies about $918–972bn using F1, which remains in rung 3.\", \"rests_on\": [\"F1\", \"e2535\", \"e1324\", \"e3645\", \"e3646\"]}\n  1681\t  - {\"id\": \"F3\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center capex\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 950.4, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"], \"operation\": \"share\"}\n  1682\t  Recomputed outcome: [950.4, 950.4, null]\n  1683\t  Recorded assumptions: [{\"type\": \"estimate\", \"figure\": \"F2\", \"assumption\": \"My estimate is that 88% of the combined capex reference is data-center capex. I use 85\\u201390% as a reasonable sensitivity range: the cited evidence is heavily focused on AI infrastructure and data-center capacity, but the reference is total capex and the passages do not quantify the data-center portion. The range implies about $918\\u2013972bn using F1, which remains in rung 3.\"}]\n  1684\t  Card K1: {\"view_id\": \"v6\", \"shape\": \"forecast\", \"rung\": 3, \"reasons\": \"I forecast about $950bn of data-center capex: the $1.08tn consensus is a useful market reference for hyperscaler capex, but it is not itself the answer because it is not data-center-only. Applying my estimated 88% data-center share gives $950.4bn; a plausible 85–90% share gives about $918–972bn, still within rung 3. NVIDIA’s $1.3tn top-five capex projection is a materially higher alternative, but it covers five companies and all capex, and is an outside projection rather than a quantified commitment of data-center spending by the four in the question. The evidence of further capex growth and capacity additions supports substantial spend, while power, grid-connection, permitting and component constraints argue against simply taking the highest projection as delivered spend. On balance, the consensus anchor adjusted to the requested scope is more likely than the high top-five projection to represent the outcome.\", \"cites\": [\"e1926\", \"e2535\", \"e1076\", \"e1324\", \"e1366\", \"e2259\", \"e3391\", \"e3645\", \"e3646\", \"e46\"], \"pushes\": [{\"evidence_id\": \"e1926\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e2535\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1076\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1324\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1366\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e2259\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e3391\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e3645\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e3646\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e46\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}], \"theme_pushes\": [], \"outcome\": \"F3\", \"reference\": {\"figure\": \"F1\"}}\n{\n  \"P07\": {\n    \"K1\": {\n      \"deciding_gap\": {\n        \"answer\": \"no\",\n        \"ground\": \"The prior-year Q4 base is missing, but holding Q4 flat at K1’s $113.6 billion Q3 estimate gives $405.02 billion for the year, inside the same band as the $411 billion consensus in e4360.\"\n      },\n      \"usable\": {\n        \"answer\": \"yes\",\n        \"ground\": \"Reported first-half sales of $177.82 billion (e97, e2710), the $113–114 billion Q3 estimate (e5088), and the $411 billion full-year consensus (e4360) support K1’s $392–431.2 billion band.\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"K2\": {\n      \"deciding_gap\": {\n        \"answer\": \"no\",\n        \"ground\": \"Using the reported $164 billion first half (e5360) and 92.5% share (e5139), flat company revenue of $113.6 billion in Q3 and Q4 gives about $374.2 billion, still within K2’s band.\"\n      },\n      \"usable\": {\n        \"answer\": \"yes\",\n        \"ground\": \"K2’s $373.4 billion forecast uses the observed segment share in e5139, and both Morningstar’s $361 billion forecast in e2295 and the expected Q4 hyperscale acceleration in e3459 support its band.\"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"P14\": {\n    \"K1\": {\n      \"contradiction\": {\n        \"answer\": \"yes\",\n        \"ground\": \"K1’s F3 forecasts $119.5 billion of FY2027 Q4 company revenue, while K2’s F11 uses $124.96 billion for that same total.\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"K2\": {\n      \"contradiction\": {\n        \"answer\": \"yes\",\n        \"ground\": \"K2 calculates Q4 Data Center sales from $124.96 billion of company revenue in F11, which exceeds K1’s forecast for the identical quantity by $5.46 billion.\"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"P21\": {\n    \"K1\": {\n      \"contradiction\": {\n        \"answer\": \"yes\",\n        \"ground\": \"K1’s F8 puts FY2027 Q4 company revenue at $124.96 billion, while K2’s F8 puts it at $127.44 billion.\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"K2\": {\n      \"contradiction\": {\n        \"answer\": \"yes\",\n        \"ground\": \"K2’s F7 forecasts 12.3% sequential growth in Q4 company revenue, while K1’s F7 forecasts 10% for the same quarter.\"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"P22\": {\n    \"K1\": {\n      \"contradiction\": {\n        \"answer\": \"yes\",\n        \"ground\": \"K1 adopts $115.6 billion as its Q4 company forecast in F10, while K2 adopts $112.2 billion for the same quarter in F9.\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"K2\": {\n      \"contradiction\": {\n        \"answer\": \"yes\",\n        \"ground\": \"K2 applies its 92.5% Data Center share to a $112.2 billion Q4 company forecast, while K1 raises that same company forecast to $115.6 billion.\"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"P23\": {\n    \"K1\": {\n      \"usable\": {\n        \"answer\": \"yes\",\n        \"ground\": \"K1’s $177.82 billion first half, $113.58 billion Q3 estimate and $112.2 billion Q4 implied by e2452 and e5162 already total $403.6 billion, inside its band even before its Q4 uplift.\"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"P27\": {\n    \"K1\": {\n      \"usable\": {\n        \"answer\": \"no\",\n        \"ground\": \"Even granting F2’s 90% share for the four companies, a 75% data-center share gives $877.5 billion, and e2535 gives no basis for favoring F4’s 82% share that lifts the forecast above $900 billion.\"\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}     1\t{\n     2\t \"ceiling\": 0.5,\n     3\t \"spent\": 0.33392953000000003,\n     4\t \"reserved\": 0.0,\n     5\t \"overrun\": false,\n     6\t \"refused\": 0,\n     7\t \"attempts\": 58,\n     8\t \"stopped\": false,\n     9\t \"stop_reason\": null,\n    10\t \"launchers\": {}\n    11\t}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'Outcome keys|balance at the end|used exactly|exactly one of a figure|never a piece|change written|normalise|not reconciled|def check|def .*evalu|def .*apply|def .*result|def .*verdict' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/{paid_gate,small*,*check*,*format*,*request*}.py(N); rg -n '## D690|## D439' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; cd poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23 && rg -n '\\\"reconciled\\\"|\"'^  \"pair-old|''^ \"ledger\"|''^  \"P(15|16|19|26|27)\"'\"' results.json reading/third.json | tail -18\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'Outcome keys|balance at the end|used exactly|exactly one of a figure|never a piece|change written|normalise|not reconciled|def check|def .*evalu|def .*apply|def .*result|def .*verdict' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/{paid_gate,small*,*check*,*format*,*request*}.py(N); rg -n '## D690|## D439' docs/DECISIONS-log.md; cd poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23 && rg -n '\\\"reconciled\\\"|\"'^  \"pair-old|''^ \"ledger\"|''^  \"P(15|16|19|26|27)\"'\"' results.json reading/third.json | tail -18\"","aggregated_output":"docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:97:O_NOTE = 'the balance at the end of FY2027, as the FY2027 annual report publishes it'\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:100:def checker(fmt):\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:168:        if missing or 'Outcome keys' not in it['user']:\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:321:                reconciled[f'pair-{draw_store}|{draw}'] = {'decision': 'not reconciled', 'members': [a.get('decision'), b.get('decision')]}\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:503:def normalise(calc, prefix=''):\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:573:    norm, rename = normalise(sub, prefix)\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:590:def packet_units(results, items):\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:769:def verdict(out, readings_path, key_dir):\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:8:Values are in the figure's unit; money is normalised to 'USD bn'.\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:243:def _evaluate_bounded(figs, refs, overrides):\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:302:def check(family, passages, calc):\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:505:            errors.append(('C5', f'{ans[\"view_id\"]}: the reference must name exactly one of a figure or unavailable'))\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:732:def publish_decision(family, calc, result):\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:794:def _evaluate(figs, refs, overrides):\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:821:def _evaluate_points(figs, refs, overrides):\n4871:## D439 · 2026-11-13 · While Alex is not active, the council decides by consensus and the loop iterates to the PRD's production goals\n10360:## D690 · 2026-11-20 · Alex chooses A: poc-v118 is the product, the research line rests until his new direction; the D681–D689 records are committed locally\nresults.json:25447:  \"pair-old-3|joint|3\": {\nresults.json:27374:  \"pair-old-3|separate-v3|1\": {\nresults.json:29061:  \"pair-old-3|separate-v3|2\": {\nresults.json:30440:  \"pair-old-3|separate-v3|3\": {\nresults.json:31418:  \"pair-old-3|separate-v35|1\": {\nresults.json:32149:  \"pair-old-3|separate-v35|2\": {\nresults.json:33373:  \"pair-old-3|separate-v35|3\": {\nresults.json:39892: \"reconciled\": {\nresults.json:39893:  \"pair-old-1|1\": {\nresults.json:39900:  \"pair-old-1|2\": {\nresults.json:39907:  \"pair-old-1|3\": {\nresults.json:39914:  \"pair-old-2|1\": {\nresults.json:39921:  \"pair-old-2|2\": {\nresults.json:40479:  \"pair-old-2|3\": {\nresults.json:40486:  \"pair-old-3|1\": {\nresults.json:40493:  \"pair-old-3|2\": {\nresults.json:40500:  \"pair-old-3|3\": {\nresults.json:40508: \"ledger\": {\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
