{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"/Users/lchingen/.local/share/uv/python/cpython-3.11-macos-aarch64-none/bin/python3 -B -c 'import json, pathlib, collections, re\ndef read(n): return json.loads(pathlib.Path(n).read_text())\na=[json.loads(l) for l in open('\\\\''answers.jsonl'\\\\'')]\np=read('\\\\''plan.json'\\\\'')\nr=read('\\\\''results.json'\\\\'')\nfor n in (24,48,72):\n b=a[n-1]; print(n,[(f['\\\\''value'\\\\''],f['\\\\''low'\\\\''],f['\\\\''high'\\\\'']) for f in b['\\\\''data'\\\\'']['\\\\''figures'\\\\''] if f['\\\\''outcome_for'\\\\'']],b['\\\\''data'\\\\'']['\\\\''answers'\\\\''][0]['\\\\''reasons'\\\\''][:630])\nfor n in ('\\\\''P06'\\\\'','\\\\''P15'\\\\'','\\\\''P16'\\\\'','\\\\''P19'\\\\'','\\\\''P23'\\\\'','\\\\''P26'\\\\'','\\\\''P27'\\\\''):\n print(n)\n for reader in ('\\\\''codex'\\\\'','\\\\''claude'\\\\''):\n  row=read('\\\\''reading/'\\\\''+reader+'\\\\''.json'\\\\'')[n]['\\\\''K1'\\\\''];print(reader,row['\\\\''deciding_gap'\\\\''], (row['\\\\''contradiction'\\\\''] if n=='\\\\''P23'\\\\'' else '\\\\'''\\\\''))\nprint('\\\\''scale'\\\\'');print(next(x for x in p if x['\\\\''unit'\\\\'']=='\\\\''pair-old-3'\\\\'')['\\\\''shown'\\\\'']['\\\\''e2754'\\\\''])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"/Users/lchingen/.local/share/uv/python/cpython-3.11-macos-aarch64-none/bin/python3 -B -c 'import json, pathlib, collections, re\ndef read(n): return json.loads(pathlib.Path(n).read_text())\na=[json.loads(l) for l in open('\\\\''answers.jsonl'\\\\'')]\np=read('\\\\''plan.json'\\\\'')\nr=read('\\\\''results.json'\\\\'')\nfor n in (24,48,72):\n b=a[n-1]; print(n,[(f['\\\\''value'\\\\''],f['\\\\''low'\\\\''],f['\\\\''high'\\\\'']) for f in b['\\\\''data'\\\\'']['\\\\''figures'\\\\''] if f['\\\\''outcome_for'\\\\'']],b['\\\\''data'\\\\'']['\\\\''answers'\\\\''][0]['\\\\''reasons'\\\\''][:630])\nfor n in ('\\\\''P06'\\\\'','\\\\''P15'\\\\'','\\\\''P16'\\\\'','\\\\''P19'\\\\'','\\\\''P23'\\\\'','\\\\''P26'\\\\'','\\\\''P27'\\\\''):\n print(n)\n for reader in ('\\\\''codex'\\\\'','\\\\''claude'\\\\''):\n  row=read('\\\\''reading/'\\\\''+reader+'\\\\''.json'\\\\'')[n]['\\\\''K1'\\\\''];print(reader,row['\\\\''deciding_gap'\\\\''], (row['\\\\''contradiction'\\\\''] if n=='\\\\''P23'\\\\'' else '\\\\'''\\\\''))\nprint('\\\\''scale'\\\\'');print(next(x for x in p if x['\\\\''unit'\\\\'']=='\\\\''pair-old-3'\\\\'')['\\\\''shown'\\\\'']['\\\\''e2754'\\\\''])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"24 [(38, None, None)] The market reference is NVIDIA's guidance that FY2028 CPU revenue will more than double the FY2027 $20 billion figure, putting its $40 billion threshold in rung 2 (F1–F3). That guidance is meaningful, but it is not evidence that the required Vera volumes are already secured. Management's broad deployment statement supports a substantial ramp, while Anthropic is only evaluating Vera and neither item sizes demand. AMD's reported competitive results are a risk, though they do not establish a revenue shortfall by themselves. On balance, I forecast $38 billion: a modest discount to the more-than-double guidance threshold, refle\n48 [(43, None, None)] The guidance-derived reference is the more-than-double expectation against $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue: its $40 billion floor is rung 2. The evidence favors rung 2 over the alternatives. Management's Vera-specific expectation and anticipated deployment across major hyperscalers, neoclouds, AI labs, and system OEMs support revenue above that floor. But this remains a forward target, not a volume-backed order forecast; Anthropic is only evaluating Vera, and AMD's reported benchmark advantages are a relevant execution and competition risk. I therefore forecast $43 billion, below the $50 billion rung-1 threshold. The bro\n72 [(37.5, None, None)] The company outlook is the reference: the cited $20bn FY2027 CPU base and more-than-double FY2028 expectation imply a $40bn lower-bound benchmark, at rung 2's lower edge. I do not take that target in full for Vera-specific FY2028 revenue: the evidence gives no Vera shipment volume or sized customer commitments, and the FY2027 base is described as CPU revenue rather than an independently quantified Vera-only base. I estimate $37.5bn after a modest execution and scope discount. Broad deployment expectations and the Anthropic evaluation support the high-$30bn outcome, while neither establishes the volume required to make $40b\nP06\ncodex {'answer': 'yes', 'ground': 'F5 leaves the overlap between F1 and F2 unresolved and gives no amount for payments or new commitments that would place the ending balance between $25 billion and $50 billion.'} \nclaude {'answer': 'yes', 'ground': 'The band rests on the unresolved overlap between F1 and F2 and on unsized fulfillment and further commitments in F5: if the $18 billion of e2889 is within the $25 billion of e2620 and is funded by year end, the balance falls toward rung 1, and the passages size none of this.'} \nP15\ncodex {'answer': 'yes', 'ground': 'F10 counts half the possible Anthropic investment without evidence for that fraction, and counting the full amount would move F14 from $45 billion to the $50 billion cutoff.'} \nclaude {'answer': 'yes', 'ground': 'The band rests on unsized adjustments: the 50% weights in F10 and F11 and the $6.75bn allowance F12 are sized by no passage, counting the full Anthropic amount in e4648 alone would lift F14 to $50bn (rung 3), and unsized fundings out of the balance could take it below the $25bn rung-1 line where F1 starts.'} \nP16\ncodex {'answer': 'yes', 'ground': 'F4 cuts growth to 90%, taking F5 below $40 billion, but no passage sizes the revenue lost to competition or deployment delays.'} \nclaude {'answer': 'yes', 'ground': \"Without F4's unsized 10-point discount, the guidance in e3660 gives more than $40bn (F3), which is rung 2; the discount alone moves K1 to $38bn (F5), rung 3.\"} \nP19\ncodex {'answer': 'yes', 'ground': 'The four companies’ data-center spending must account for at least five-sixths of F1 to reach $900 billion, and no passage establishes that share.'} \nclaude {'answer': 'yes', 'ground': 'F3 rests on the unsized 88% data-center share F2 that no passage sizes or bounds; a share below about 83% applied to F1 would put the outcome in rung 2, and the count of hyperscalers behind e1926 versus the four asked is left unresolved.'} \nP23\ncodex {'answer': 'no', 'ground': 'Using the Q3 guidance midpoint from e3346 and $112.2 billion implied by e5162 for Q4 gives $398.02 billion, still above the $392 billion cutoff.'} {'answer': 'no', 'ground': 'K1’s Q3 and Q4 company forecasts of $113.58 billion and $116.4 billion round to K2’s $113.6 billion and $116 billion at the precision K2 reports.'}\nclaude {'answer': 'no', 'ground': 'With Q3 at the $108bn guidance (e3346) and Q4 at the consensus-derived $112.2bn (e2452, e5162) the total is about $398bn, still rung 2, so the unsized Q4 uplift does not decide the band; the 70% full-year growth figure (e3280 on e4273, $367bn) is weighed openly against reported H1 and Q3 guidance rather than left unresolved.'} {'answer': 'no', 'ground': \"K1's prose Q3 ($113.58bn) and Q4 ($116.4bn) match K2's F5 ($113.6bn) and F12 ($116bn) within rounding, and K2's Data Center F15 ($377bn) is about 92% of K1's $407.8bn, so there is no hard contradiction.\"}\nP26\ncodex {'answer': 'yes', 'ground': 'An Oracle deduction of $100 billion or less puts F3 at or above $1.2 trillion, while more than $200 billion puts it below $1.1 trillion, and the passages do not bound F2 between those amounts.'} \nclaude {'answer': 'yes', 'ground': 'The Oracle deduction F2 is neither sized nor bounded by any passage, and the card says that above $200bn it would drop to rung 3 and below $100bn it would rise to rung 5, so this unsized adjustment decides the rung-4 band of F3.'} \nP27\ncodex {'answer': 'yes', 'ground': 'Even holding F2 fixed, a 75% data-center share in F4 gives $877.5 billion and a 95% share gives $1,111.5 billion, and the passages do not establish which band is likelier.'} \nclaude {'answer': 'yes', 'ground': 'F5 rests on two shares that no passage sizes or bounds, 90% (F2) and 82% (F4); if their product falls below about 69% (for example 90% x 77%), the result drops under $900bn, and the card concedes those shares decide the band.'} \nscale\n- e2754 [fact; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from extra:2799d8731e89b90c9bd3f108 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported revenue of $177.837 billion for the first six months of FY2027, versus $90.805 billion in the same period of FY2026.\n    Quote: Revenue\n$\n96,221\n$\n46,743\n$\n177,837\n$\n90,805\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
