{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'single-O|Outcome keys .*equity investment commitments' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan-v13s.json | cut -c 1-130 | head; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py | sed -n '101,115p;178,184p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py | sed -n '79,110p;551,567p'; rg -n '\"'^  \"(P06|P05|P23|P15|P27)\"'\"' poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/reading/{readings,codex,claude}.json; rg -n 'P14|P21|P22|P23|P27' poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/reading/third.json; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '10997,11007p;11765,11775p'; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/packets.md | sed -n '38,53p' | cut -c1-290\"","aggregated_output":"24429:  \"unit\": \"single-O\",\n24437:  \"user\": \"[Family] members:\\nThesis v29 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"NVIDIA's committed equity investments in ecosystem partners\\\"\n   101\t                 '1200; $500 million = 0.5). %: a percentage as its number (7.5% = 7.5). x: a multiple (double = 2).'},\n   102\t        'evidence_id': nullable({'type': 'string', 'enum': evidence_ids, 'description': 'Source only: the passage whose quote holds the number.'}),\n   103\t        'quote_number': nullable({'type': 'string', 'description': \"Source only: a complete number exactly as the quote writes it, never a \"\n   104\t                                  \"piece of a longer number, range or 'plus or minus' expression, with its currency and scale or its percent \"\n   105\t                                  \"sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', \"\n   106\t                                  \"'double', 'twice'. The value is the number as written (a minus only when the quote writes one). A written \"\n   107\t                                  \"range in full ('$3–4 billion', 'between $3 billion and $4 billion', '$3 billion to $4 billion'), given as low and high; a change \"\n   108\t                                  \"written 'from $3 billion to $5 billion' is two numbers, each copied alone. '$4.5 billion, \"\n   109\t                                  \"plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value. Only US dollars.\"}),\n   110\t        'qualifier': nullable({'type': 'string', 'enum': QUALIFIERS, 'description': \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before \"\n   111\t                               \"the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\"}),\n   112\t        'inputs': nullable({'type': 'array', 'items': S, 'description': \"Computed only: ids of figures, never passages, in the operation's order.\"}),\n   113\t        'operation': nullable({'type': 'string', 'enum': ['sum', 'difference', 'product', 'ratio', 'share', 'growth', 'apply_growth'],\n   114\t                               'description': 'Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); '\n   115\t                               'product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units '\n   178\t    shared = '; '.join(f'{w[\"population\"]}, {w[\"measure\"]}, {w[\"scope\"]}, {w[\"period\"]}' for w in family.get('shared', [])) or 'none'\n   179\t    lines.append(f'Shared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, '\n   180\t                 f'the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: {shared}.')\n   181\t    keys = '; '.join(f'{v} = {m[\"what\"][\"population\"]} | {m[\"what\"][\"measure\"]} | {m[\"what\"][\"scope\"]} | {m[\"what\"][\"period\"]}'\n   182\t                     + (f' ({m[\"key_note\"]})' if m.get('key_note') else '') for v, m in family.get('members', {}).items() if v in shown)\n   183\t    lines.append(f'Outcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: {keys}.')\n   184\t    return '\\n'.join(lines) + '\\n'\n    79\t\n    80\t_CHANGE = re.compile(r'(?<!rang)(?<!range)(?<!ranges)(?<!ranging)\\bfrom\\s*$', re.I)\n    81\t\n    82\t\n    83\tdef expressions(text):\n    84\t    \"\"\"[54] Every complete numeric expression in a quote, as (start, end), longest forms first and never overlapping. [D740] An\n    85\t    'X to Y' written after 'from' (not 'range(s) from' or 'ranging from') is a change between two values, so its two amounts are\n    86\t    separate expressions.\"\"\"\n    87\t    taken = []\n    88\t    for form, pattern in _SCAN:\n    89\t        for m in pattern.finditer(text):\n    90\t            if form == 'range' and re.search(r'\\bto\\b', m.group(0), re.I) and not m.group(0).lower().startswith('between'):\n    91\t                before = text[:m.start()].rstrip().lower()\n    92\t                if before.endswith('from') and not re.search(r'\\b(range|ranges|ranged|ranging)\\s+from$', before):\n    93\t                    continue\n    94\t            if not any(m.start() < e and s < m.end() for s, e in taken):\n    95\t                taken.append((m.start(), m.end()))\n    96\t    return sorted(taken)\n    97\t\n    98\t\n    99\tdef _key(what):\n   100\t    return (what['population'], what['measure'], what['scope'], what['period'])\n   101\t\n   102\t\n   103\tdef _quantity(what):\n   104\t    return (what['population'], what['measure'], what['scope'])\n   105\t\n   106\t\n   107\tdef _same(a, b):\n   108\t    return a is not None and b is not None and abs(a - b) <= 1e-9 * max(1.0, abs(a), abs(b))\n   109\t\n   110\t\n   551\t    errors, outcomes = [], {}\n   552\t    members = family['members']\n   553\t    for a in answers:\n   554\t        v = a.get('view_id')\n   555\t        member = members.get(v)\n   556\t        if member is None or a.get('shape') == 'unresolved':\n   557\t            continue\n   558\t        out = figs.get(a.get('outcome'))\n   559\t        if out is None:\n   560\t            continue                                       # reported by from_answer\n   561\t        if out['kind'] not in ('computed', 'estimate') or out['basis'] != 'forecast':\n   562\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: its outcome {out[\"id\"]} must be your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast)'))\n   563\t        if _key(out['what']) != _key(member['what']):\n   564\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: outcome {out[\"id\"]} measures {\" | \".join(_key(out[\"what\"]))}, not the question\\'s {\" | \".join(_key(member[\"what\"]))}'))\n   565\t        if out['unit'] != member.get('unit', 'USD bn'):\n   566\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: outcome {out[\"id\"]} is in {out[\"unit\"]}, not {member.get(\"unit\", \"USD bn\")}'))\n   567\t        outcomes[v] = out['id']\npoc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/reading/readings.json:202:  \"P05\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/reading/readings.json:280:  \"P06\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/reading/readings.json:976:  \"P15\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/reading/readings.json:1510:  \"P23\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/reading/readings.json:1790:  \"P27\": {\n24:  \"P14\": {\n38:  \"P21\": {\n52:  \"P22\": {\n66:  \"P23\": {\n74:  \"P27\": {\n 10997\t  - \"第一版上线前的过渡：数字题只显示问题和来源，档位和预测文字写成'预测暂缺'，等通过检查再放出来。你同意这个做法吗？\" Options: 同意\n 10998\t    (recommended); 暂不定.\n 10999\t- **Alex's answers** (2026-11-22): \"开，先做 0 元那一关 (推荐)\"; \"同意 (推荐)\".\n 11000\t- **Decided (Alex):**\n 11001\t  - A new judge version is opened for the bounded test of D725: related number questions judged together, with labelled figures and\n 11002\t    code checks of identity and arithmetic. It is designed and reviewed at $0, then the $0 gate runs. The paid gate needs Alex's\n 11003\t    ceiling.\n 11004\t  - Until the number answers pass, a first release shows number questions with their sources and \"Forecast unavailable\": the band, the\n 11005\t    forecast text and its history withheld together. It is built with the release work, which is not started.\n 11006\t- poc-v118 stays the product.\n 11007\t\n 11765\t     - \"先停，我想谈谈\".\n 11766\t- **Alex's answers** (2026-11-23): \"显示“暂时判断不了” (推荐)\"; \"破例一次，付费重发\".\n 11767\t- **Decided (Alex):**\n 11768\t  1. When a reaffirmed target sits near a band line and, after weighing it with the other evidence, neither band is more likely, the\n 11769\t     system shows \"can't tell yet\" with the target as context and names the deciding fact. The request already states it (line 9);\n 11770\t     nothing changes.\n 11771\t  2. A one-time exception to D749's clean-pass condition: the paid re-send of the 36 requests, rebuilt in v13s with the D751 request,\n 11772\t     under D725's lines and the paid gate's reading rule (`PAID-GATE.md` §4), with a total cap of $0.50 including diagnostic\n 11773\t     retries. It is prepared at $0 and reviewed by the council before the send.\n 11774\t\n 11775\t## D754 · 2026-11-23 · The one-time v13s paid re-send is prepared, reviewed in council 70–70c, and sent under Alex's D753\n    38\t    Quote: In a modeled 100-kilowatt rack, it delivers an estimated 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    39\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5444; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    40\t  - e5402 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores had a 37% performance gain over NVIDIA Vera, while its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores had a 2.37-times gain \n    41\t    Quote: Here, the 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores provide a 37% gain over NVIDIA Vera & the 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores provides a 2.37x gain.\n    42\t  - e5403 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9006 server CPUs averaged 12% higher per-core performance in Agentic AI-specific benchmarks, with a4 subtests averaging 20%.\n    43\t    Quote: Moving over to Agentic AI-specific benchmark tests, AMD's EPYC 9006 Server CPUs drive a 12% gain in per-core performance benchmarks, with the a4 subtests' performance averaging at 20%.\n    44\t  - e5404 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that EPYC Venice had 18% faster memory bandwidth than NVIDIA Vera, with 1.6 TB/s versus Vera’s 1.2 TB/s, in STREAM Triad testing.\n    45\t    Quote: AMD's EPYC Venice delivered 18% faster memory bandwidth versus NVIDIA's Vera thanks to its 16-channel memory design, which supports up to 12,800 MT/s RDIMM modules. This provides the platform with 1.6 TB/s of memory bandwidth versus 1.2 TB/s bandwidth on the NVIDIA Vera C\n    46\t  Figures (declared):\n    47\t  - {\"id\": \"F1\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 20, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"quote_number\": \"$20 billion\", \"evidence\": \"e3660\"}\n    48\t  - {\"id\": \"F2\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue multiple\", \"scope\": \"FY2028 CPU revenue relative to FY2027 CPU revenue\", \"period\": \"FY2028 vs FY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 2, \"unit\": \"x\", \"quote_number\": \"double\", \"qualifier\": \"more \n    49\t  - {\"id\": \"F3\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"}, \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 40, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"], \"operation\": \"product\"}\n    50\t  - {\"id\": \"F4\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"}, \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 43, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"basis_text\": \"My point estimate is $43 billion: modestly above the $40 billion floor implied by \n    51\t  Recomputed outcome: [43.0, 43.0, null]\n    52\t  Recorded assumptions: [{\"type\": \"qualified source\", \"figure\": \"F2\", \"qualifier\": \"more than\"}, {\"type\": \"conversion between quantities\", \"figure\": \"F3\"}, {\"type\": \"carried across periods\", \"figure\": \"F3\"}, {\"type\": \"estimate\", \"figure\": \"F4\", \"assumption\": \"My point estimate is $4\n    53\t  Card K1: {\"view_id\": \"v41\", \"shape\": \"forecast\", \"rung\": 2, \"reasons\": \"The guidance-derived reference is the more-than-double expectation against $20 billion of FY2027 CPU revenue: its $40 billion floor is rung 2. The evidence favors rung 2 over the alternatives. Management's Ver\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\\\"end of FY2027\\\"|\\\"key_note\\\"' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan-v13s.json | tail -6; rg -n 'Revenue\"'$|NVIDIA reported revenue of '\"\\\\\"'$177'\"\\\\.837|quote writes|e2207.*transaction includes' poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/packets.md | cut -c 1-150 | head -11; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/reading/third.json | tail -19; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11728,11740p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\\\"end of FY2027\\\"|\\\"key_note\\\"' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan-v13s.json | tail -6; rg -n 'Revenue\"'$|NVIDIA reported revenue of '\"\\\\\"'$177'\"\\\\.837|quote writes|e2207.*transaction includes' poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/packets.md | cut -c 1-150 | head -11; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/reading/third.json | tail -19; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11728,11740p'\"","aggregated_output":"24956:      \"period\": \"end of FY2027\"\n24981:     \"key_note\": \"the balance at the end of FY2027, as the FY2027 annual report publishes it\"\n252:  - e2207 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA and Poolside, as reported by Newcomer based on a Poolside investor letter 2026-08-20; f\n497:  - e2754 [fact; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from extra:2799d8731e89b90c9bd3f108 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported revenue of $177.837 bi\n498:    Quote: Revenue\n821:  - e2754 [fact; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from extra:2799d8731e89b90c9bd3f108 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported revenue of $177.837 bi\n822:    Quote: Revenue\n1078:  - e2754 [fact; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from extra:2799d8731e89b90c9bd3f108 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported revenue of $177.837 b\n1079:    Quote: Revenue\n1189:  - e2754 [fact; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from extra:2799d8731e89b90c9bd3f108 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported revenue of $177.837 b\n1190:    Quote: Revenue\n    64\t    }\n    65\t  },\n    66\t  \"P23\": {\n    67\t    \"K1\": {\n    68\t      \"usable\": {\n    69\t        \"answer\": \"yes\",\n    70\t        \"ground\": \"K1’s $177.82 billion first half, $113.58 billion Q3 estimate and $112.2 billion Q4 implied by e2452 and e5162 already total $403.6 billion, inside its band even before its Q4 uplift.\"\n    71\t      }\n    72\t    }\n    73\t  },\n    74\t  \"P27\": {\n    75\t    \"K1\": {\n    76\t      \"usable\": {\n    77\t        \"answer\": \"no\",\n    78\t        \"ground\": \"Even granting F2’s 90% share for the four companies, a 75% data-center share gives $877.5 billion, and e2535 gives no basis for favoring F4’s 82% share that lifts the forecast above $900 billion.\"\n    79\t      }\n    80\t    }\n    81\t  }\n    82\t} 11728\t    - S Codex: supported;\n 11729\t    - both P-v8 forecasts: supported;\n 11730\t    - both controls: caught.\n 11731\t  - Split: S Claude's $20B deduction.\n 11732\t- **The record across the five runs** (`BLIND7-RESULT.md` §2):\n 11733\t  - The Codex writer invented cuts twice, then gave three justified \"can't tell\" answers on E, each accepted by both families.\n 11734\t  - The Claude writer's five E forecasts never earned settled support (four settled no, one split).\n 11735\t  - P-v8 was supported in every run, and the controls were caught 6 of 6 since D749. S is mixed.\n 11736\t  - Only the paid gate compares joint against reconciled separate pairs, D725's hypothesis. No free test can answer it.\n 11737\t- **The exit rule fired** (council 67, registered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D751]). The wording loop ends, and the publication choice\n 11738\t  for E-type cases returns to Alex.\n 11739\t- **Council round 69** (`codex-r69.md`) and **69b** (`codex-r69b.md`):\n 11740\t  - **Agreed, question 1.** When a reaffirmed target sits near a band line and, after weighing it with the other evidence, neither\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"agent_message","text":"**The STOP is right. Keep “Forecast unavailable” for number questions.** The test required eight usable pairs and produced three. It also published five forecasts whose chosen range depended on a number the sources did not support. Either result was enough to stop. The result page is right about the verdict, but it assigns too many refusals to Luna. [PAID-GATE.md:270](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:270) · [verdict.json:7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/verdict.json:7)\n\n1. **The 22 refusals are real. One was caused solely by a mismatch between the request and the checker.** On the first equity-commitments answer, the request said to copy the period exactly and displayed `end of FY2027` followed by an explanatory parenthesis. Luna copied both. The checker expected only `end of FY2027`. In a read-only replay, removing just the parenthesis changed the decision from refused to published. Both readers had independently found that this same $45B forecast depended on unresolved overlap, so admitting it would have exposed another defective forecast. [Paid plan:24437](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan-v13s.json:24437) · [Expected period:24956](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid-plan-v13s.json:24956) · [answers.jsonl:20](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/answers.jsonl:20) · [P06 readings:280](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/reading/readings.json:280)\n\n   I checked every refusal against the saved request. Each first attempt appears once below. The line numbers refer to `answers.jsonl`.\n\n   | Main reason | Answers | Lines |\n   |---|---:|---|\n   | Copied only part of the `$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%` guidance | 7 | [6](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/answers.jsonl:6), 10, 14, 30, 42, 54, 66 |\n   | Marked several references | 6 | [16](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/answers.jsonl:16), 28, 40, 52, 64, 72. Lines 28 and 52 also marked several outcomes. |\n   | Marked several outcomes for both questions | 1 | [62](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/answers.jsonl:62) |\n   | Put a qualifier inside the quoted number | 4 | [32](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/answers.jsonl:32), 38, 56, 60. Line 32 also changed `over` to `more than`. |\n   | Rewrote a quoted range, or took `$1 billion` from the summary when the supplied quote omitted it | 2 | [50](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/answers.jsonl:50), [68](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/answers.jsonl:68) |\n   | Supplied both a point and a range | 1 | [58](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/answers.jsonl:58) |\n   | Copied the period as displayed | 1 | [20](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/answers.jsonl:20) |\n\n   Two other corrections matter. **Three answers** marked several outcomes. The page reports four. And on lines 14 and 38, the checker mistook a change **over time** for a range. The source goes from $253B in trailing revenue to $392B **this fiscal year**, then $554B the following year. Luna correctly took $392B for the current year. Both answers had another genuine error. Both also hit the split table number. Joining `$` to `177,837` alone still fails because the quoted table omits its scale. [Result page:33](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-V13S-RESULT.md:33) · [Source passage:489](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/packets.md:489) · [Table:497](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/packets.md:497) · [Request’s rule for changes:107](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py:107)\n\n   The defensible headline is **“the checker refused 22/36 first attempts (61.1%) on these 12 requests.”** Of those, 21 had an identifiable violation by Luna and one had only the period mismatch. Eight of the 22 retries cleared the checks, including six separate answers, two single-question answers and zero joint answers. Retries were diagnostic. [Result page:17](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-V13S-RESULT.md:17) · [retries.jsonl:2](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/retries.jsonl:2) · [First-attempt rule:11090](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11090)\n\n2. **All five published defects hold up.** Both original readers marked the unsupported adjustment in each case. The third reader settled a separate disagreement about P27’s overall usability. These are five of six published answers to **three selected questions**, including three attempts at the same spending question. [Verdict:16](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/verdict.json:16) · [P27 readings:1790](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/reading/readings.json:1790) · [Third reading:74](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/reading/third.json:74)\n\n   | Forecast | What changes the answer |\n   |---|---|\n   | P15 · Equity commitments | Luna counted half of a possible $10B Anthropic investment. Counting the full amount moves $45B to the $50B boundary. The source gives neither signing odds nor a date. [Packet:1344](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/packets.md:1344) |\n   | P16 · Vera CPU revenue | Starting from $20B, doubling reaches $40B. Luna chose 90% growth and got $38B. The cited competition and deployment risks do not size that cut. [Packet:1401](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/packets.md:1401) |\n   | P19 · Data-center spending | Reaching $900B from $1.08T requires at least five-sixths of the total. Luna chose 88% and tested 85–90%. The evidence does not establish even the lower end. [Packet:1680](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/packets.md:1680) |\n   | P26 · Data-center spending | Luna deducted $150B for Oracle from $1.3T. A deduction of $100B or less reaches the higher range. More than $200B reaches the lower one. The source does not bound Oracle’s spending. [Packet:2401](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/packets.md:2401) |\n   | P27 · Data-center spending | Even granting Luna’s first assumption, a 75% data-center share gives $877.5B and 95% gives $1,111.5B. The sources give no reason to favor the 82% used for publication. [Packet:2448](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/packets.md:2448) |\n\n   Luna gave a number on all three Vera attempts. The readers accepted the $43B answer because the evidence supported its range. The $38B and $37.5B answers crossed below $40B through unsupported cuts. The earlier free tests had already exposed that behavior, although they did not establish its frequency for Luna. [Accepted answer:49](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/packets.md:49) · [Third attempt:72](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/answers.jsonl:72) · [Earlier tests:11732](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11732)\n\n3. **“Three joint versus zero separate” is correct. The reason needs more care.** The joint results were 2/3, 1/3 and 0/3 across the three snapshots. The gain of three met that one requirement. Total coverage, coverage per snapshot and the zero-defect requirement all failed. Of the nine separate pairs, **eight never reached the comparison** because at least one answer had already been refused. Only one pair reached it. Its two answers used $113.5B versus $108B for the same Q3 company revenue, and $135.065B versus $123.12B for Q4. [Verdict:9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/verdict.json:9) · [Comparison rule:319](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:319) · [Separate results:39892](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/results.json:39892) · [Compared pair:39921](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/results.json:39921)\n\n   There **is** directional evidence for doing shared arithmetic once. Readers saw even the withheld answers. Their settled readings found conflicting numbers in **eight of nine separate pairs and zero of nine joint pairs**. In the ninth separate pair, both readers found that rounding explained the difference. That supports D725’s claim about consistency on these snapshots. The experiment did not establish forecast accuracy or readiness to publish. [Reading rule:259](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:259) · [Settled readings:202](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/reading/readings.json:202) · [Third-reader examples:24](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/reading/third.json:24) · [Rounding case:1510](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/reading/readings.json:1510)\n\n4. **What each production option buys.**\n\n   | Option | Cost and decision |\n   |---|---|\n   | Close v13s and use Alex’s first-release fallback | **$0 tonight.** With the release work, show the question and sources with “Forecast unavailable.” Withhold the range, forecast text and history together. This protects readers while leaving the numerical question open. `poc-v118` remains the product. [D726:11004](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11004) |\n   | Try higher effort or a stronger model on number questions | **A new paid experiment**, with a new ceiling set by Alex. This run cost $0.33393 for 58 attempts. It does not price another configuration. A fresh test could measure whether the model follows the format and says “can’t tell yet” when the evidence cannot choose a range. A stronger model still cannot supply a missing order volume or spending share. [Ledger:2](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/ledger.json:2) · [Alex’s publication rule:11768](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11768) |\n   | Repair how code reads the format | **$0 in model calls for a stored-answer check**, plus engineering and independent testing if Alex reopens the work. In memory, separating the qualifier from the amount cleared one joint answer and one separate answer. Joining the table’s line break cleared neither affected answer. These changes can recover correctly expressed numbers. They cannot justify any of the five published forecasts. The registered STOP sends the work back to Alex and bars tuning on these 36 answers. [Joint answer:56](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/answers.jsonl:56) · [Separate answer:60](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-paid-2026-11-23/answers.jsonl:60) · [PAID-GATE.md:278](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:278) |\n\n**Recommendation for Alex’s morning:** Close v13s at STOP and carry D726 into the first release. The one next step, when Alex opens release work, is a **$0 check of the reader’s screen** using a stored number question with a forecast and history. The reader should see the question, its sources and “Forecast unavailable,” with the old range, forecast text and history all withheld. Tonight’s review changed no files and spent $0. Any further paid send, effort/model/provider change or new judge version requires Alex’s authorization. [D726:11004](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11004) · [One-time authorization:11771](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11771)"}}
